From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 19:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] rocket: do not free the task array twice

rocket_copy_tasks() allocates rjob->tasks and, on any failure inside its
copy loop, frees it again on the fail: path -- without clearing the
pointer.  Its only caller, rocket_ioctl_submit_job(), then unwinds
through rocket_job_put() -> rocket_job_cleanup(), which frees
job->tasks unconditionally.  The same allocation goes back to the
allocator twice.

Two failures reach it, and both are ordinary userspace input: the task
copy fails (a bad user pointer, or with 085 a trailing field this kernel
does not know), or task.regcmd_count is 0.  The second is not
hypothetical -- a userspace whose register-program generator REFUSES a
shape leaves regcmd_count at its zeroed value, and a caller that submits
anyway lands exactly here.

/dev/accel/accel0 is group `render`, so this is an unprivileged double
free of a kmalloc-16 object (a struct rocket_task is 16 bytes), one of
the hottest caches in the kernel.  The freelist then hands one object to
two owners and the damage surfaces far from here, in whatever allocates
next.

VERIFIED ON THE RK3576, where this function is byte-identical, with
slub_debug=FZPU slab_nomerge: two "BUG kmalloc-16: Object already free"
reports per run of a client that submits rejected jobs -- naming
rocket_ioctl_submit() as the allocation site and rocket_job_cleanup() as
the second free -- before, and zero after, over ten rounds of the full
gate sequence on a clean boot.  The two faults it was chased through
were a scatterlist walked in drm_gem_shmem_release() from
rocket_gem_bo_free() and a wild phys_to_virt() inside swiotlb_bounce()
reached from drm_gem_shmem_get_pages_sgt(); neither is a defect in those
paths and neither reproduces on the fixed module.  NOT YET EXERCISED ON
RK3588 SILICON -- the code, the reachability and the fix are identical,
but the A/B above is the RK3576's.

Fix it by giving the array a single owner.  rocket_job_cleanup() already
frees it on every path out of the ioctl, including the ones that never
reach the hardware, so the fail: path only has to stop.

This is mainline code; the RK3576 series carries the same fix as 0020.

Signed-off-by: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c | 6 +++++-
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
index 93b994909..260285bcc 100644
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
@@ -152,7 +152,11 @@ rocket_copy_tasks(struct drm_device *dev,
 	return 0;
 
 fail:
-	kvfree(rjob->tasks);
+	/*
+	 * Do NOT free rjob->tasks here.  Every caller unwinds through
+	 * rocket_job_cleanup(), which frees it unconditionally, so freeing it
+	 * on this path hands the same object to the allocator twice.
+	 */
 	return ret;
 }
 
